from pathlib import Path
from html import escape
ROOT=Path(__file__).parent
PAGES=[('index','Home'),('about','About'),('cfp','Call for Papers'),('program','Program'),('keynotes','Keynotes'),('organization','Organization'),('autumn-school','Autumn School'),('registration','Registration'),('local-information','Local Information')]
dates=[('Full papers due','April 29, 2027'),('Author response period','June 28 – 30, 2027'),('Author notification','July 22, 2027'),('Camera-ready deadline','August 22, 2027'),('Main conference','November 2 – 3, 2027')]
def date_section():
 return '<section id="important-dates"><h2>Important Dates</h2><p class="note">All deadlines are 23:59 AoE (Anywhere on Earth).</p><dl class="dates">'+''.join(f'<div class="date-row"><dt>{a}</dt><dd>{b}</dd></div>' for a,b in dates)+'</dl></section>'
home='''<div class="actions"><a class="action" href="cfp.html"><strong>Call for Papers ↗</strong><span>Full papers due April 29, 2027</span></a><a class="action" href="program.html"><strong>Main Conference ↗</strong><span>November 2 – 3, 2027</span></a><a class="action" href="registration.html"><strong>Registration ↗</strong><span>Details to be announced</span></a></div><section><h2 style="margin-top:0">Building AI We Can Trust</h2><div class="prose"><p>AI is becoming more capable every year, and making it safe matters more than ever. For instance, AI agents can find and exploit vulnerabilities at scale, launch cyber attacks of their own, or write code that hides subtle safety bugs and security flaws. As AI moves into the physical world, through autonomous vehicles, robots, and the systems behind critical infrastructure, unsafe behaviour can have real-world consequences.</p><p>SAIV 2027 brings together researchers in AI and formal methods, but also related fields such as cybersecurity, software engineering, programming languages, control theory, or robotics that work on aspects of AI verification, to tackle the AI safety challenge. Topics of interest include AI-assisted code generation and translation that is safe by construction and backed by formal proofs of correctness; autoformalisation, which turns informal requirements into precise, machine-checkable proofs; formal explainability, which provides rigorous, provably correct explanations of AI decisions; runtime monitoring and safeguarding, which detect and correct unsafe behaviour before it causes harm; robustness against attacks and data poisoning; world models, which give AI systems an explicit, analysable representation of their environment so that the consequences of their actions can be predicted and verified before they are taken; and safety assurance for physical AI and robotics systems. Underpinning all of this is the development of formal specifications that capture precisely what it means for an AI system to be safe, secure, and correct.</p></div></section>'''+date_section()+'''<section><h2>Quick Links</h2><div class="quick"><div><h3>Authors</h3><a href="cfp.html">Call for Papers</a><a href="cfp.html#submission-guidelines">Submission Guidelines</a></div><div><h3>Program</h3><a href="program.html">Program Overview</a><a href="keynotes.html">Keynotes</a><a href="autumn-school.html">Autumn School</a></div><div><h3>Attend</h3><a href="registration.html">Registration</a><a href="local-information.html">Local Information</a><a href="organization.html">Organization</a></div></div></section>'''
about='''<h2>A new chapter for AI verification</h2><div class="prose"><p>SAIV 2027 will be the first instance of SAIV organized as an independent conference, and our goal is to grow SAIV into a premier venue for AI safety research. The conference builds on a 9-year legacy as the successor to the SAIV workshop, and previously FoMLAS and WOLVERINE workshops, that were co-organized with the International Conference on Computer-Aided Verification (CAV). Given the growing importance of AI safety and trustworthiness, SAIV will be organized as an independent annual conference starting 2027.</p></div>'''
cfp='''<div class="prose"><p>SAIV 2027 invites regular paper submissions on AI verification. The list of topics falling under the scope of SAIV 2027 is provided below. Papers should be original work that have not been published at or are under submission to another peer-reviewed venue; see the <a href="#dual-submission">dual submission policy</a> below. We also invite presentations of recently published or ongoing work at our <a href="#research-highlights">AI Safety Research Highlights</a> event.</p></div>'''+date_section()+'''<section id="subject-areas"><h2>Subject Areas</h2><div class="prose"><p>SAIV 2027 is a conference dedicated to the advancement of AI with rigorous formal guarantees. We invite submissions from all researchers, irrespective of area boundaries, who use logical and mathematical methods to establish or assess the safety, alignment, security, privacy, and interpretability of AI, as well as work that uses AI to advance formal reasoning itself. By AI, we refer not only to learned neural models but also to the digital agents and physical robots powered by them. Topics of interest include, but are not limited to:</p></div><ul class="topics"><li>Formal specifications for AI</li><li>Neuro-symbolic architectures with formal guarantees</li><li>Formal methods for AI, including verification, testing, falsification, probabilistic analysis, and runtime monitoring:<ul><li>Verifying neural models, from small edge models to foundation and world models</li><li>Verifying agents and physical robots</li><li>Formal explainability and interpretability</li></ul></li><li>AI for formal methods:<ul><li>Autoformalization</li><li>Formal theorem proving</li><li>Verified code generation and translation</li></ul></li></ul></section><section id="submission-guidelines"><h2>Submission Guidelines</h2><p>To be announced.</p></section><section><h2>Conference Proceedings</h2><p>Papers selected for publication will appear in the SAIV 2027 conference proceedings in the LNCS series.</p></section><section><h2>Distinguished Paper Awards</h2><p>Up to 10% of accepted papers may be awarded the Distinguished Paper Award.</p></section><section id="dual-submission"><h2>Dual Submission Policy</h2><p>A submitted paper should be original work that has not been published and is not under submission to another peer-reviewed venue. However, works that have appeared on archival websites (such as arXiv) or that have been presented at venues that do not have publication proceedings (such as workshops without proceedings) can be submitted to SAIV 2027. The dual submission policy is enforced during the whole reviewing process period. Submission of papers to archival repositories such as arXiv is allowed during the review process.</p></section><section><h2>AI Use Policy</h2><p>The use of AI in submission preparation is allowed, but should be disclosed in the paper under the “AI Use Statement”, which does not count towards the page limit. However, authors remain fully responsible for the content of their papers. Any instances of plagiarism, non-existent references, and other cases of LLM hallucinations or bloat will lead to desk rejection.</p></section><section id="research-highlights"><h2>AI Safety Research Highlights</h2><p>Presentations of recently published or ongoing work are welcome. Event details and submission instructions will be announced.</p></section>'''
roles=[('General Chair',[('Mirco Giacobbe','University of Birmingham, UK')]),('Program Chairs',[('Corina S. Pasareanu','Carnegie Mellon University & Amazon, USA'),('Djordje Zikelic','Nanyang Technological University, Singapore')]),('Local Chairs',[('Pian Yu','University College London, UK')]),('Website Chair',[('Jingbo Wang','Purdue University, USA')]),('Publicity Chair',[('Ravi Mangal','Colorado State University, USA')]),('Autumn School',[]),('Steering Committee',[]),('Board of Advisors',[])]
organization=''.join('<section><h2>'+role+'</h2>'+('<div class="people">'+''.join(f'<div class="person"><strong>{escape(name)}</strong><span>{escape(affiliation)}</span></div>' for name,affiliation in people)+'</div>' if people else '<p class="note">To be announced.</p>')+'</section>' for role,people in roles)
def pending(extra=''):
 return '<div class="pending"><span class="status">SAIV 2027</span><h2>To be announced.</h2>'+extra+'<p class="note">Further details will be published here.</p><a href="index.html">← Back to homepage</a></div>'
content={'index':home,'about':about,'cfp':cfp,'organization':organization,'program':pending('<p>The main conference will take place on <strong>November 2 – 3, 2027</strong>.</p>'),'keynotes':pending(),'autumn-school':pending(),'registration':pending(),'local-information':pending('<p>SAIV 2027 will take place in <strong>London, UK</strong>, October 31 – November 5, 2027.</p>')}
hero='''<header class="hero"><div class="hero-copy"><p class="eyebrow">SAIV 2027 · 10th edition</p><h1>The 10th International Symposium on AI Verification</h1><p class="tagline">Building AI We Can Trust</p><div class="dateline"><a href="program.html"><strong>Oct 31 – Nov 5</strong><small>2027</small></a><a href="local-information.html"><strong>London</strong><small>United Kingdom</small></a></div></div><div class="hero-art"><img src="assets/london.svg" width="650" height="600" alt="Illustration of London's Elizabeth Tower, River Thames, and London Eye"><span class="art-caption">London, United Kingdom</span></div></header>'''
for slug,label in PAGES:
 nav=''.join(f'<li><a href="{s}.html"'+(' aria-current="page"' if s==slug else '')+f'>{l}</a></li>' for s,l in PAGES)
 header=hero if slug=='index' else f'<header class="page-head"><p class="eyebrow">SAIV 2027 · London</p><h1>{label}</h1><p>October 31 – November 5, 2027</p></header>'
 html=f'''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>{'SAIV 2027 — AI Verification' if slug=='index' else label+' — SAIV 2027'}</title><meta name="description" content="The 10th International Symposium on AI Verification. London, UK, October 31 – November 5, 2027. Building AI We Can Trust."><meta name="theme-color" content="#123247"><link rel="icon" href="assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="stylesheet" href="assets/style.css"><script defer src="assets/site.js"></script><noscript><style>@media(max-width:700px){{.nav-wrap nav{{display:block}}.menu-button{{display:none}}}}</style></noscript></head><body><a class="skip" href="#main-content">Skip to main content</a><div class="topbar"><div class="nav-wrap"><a class="brand" href="index.html">SAIV <span>2027</span></a><button class="menu-button" aria-controls="navigation" aria-expanded="false" type="button">Menu</button><nav id="navigation" aria-label="Primary navigation"><ul>{nav}</ul></nav></div></div>{header}<main id="main-content"><article class="content">{content[slug]}</article></main><footer class="footer"><div class="footer-inner"><div><a class="brand" href="index.html">SAIV <span>2027</span></a><p>The 10th International Symposium on AI Verification</p><p>October 31 – November 5, 2027 · London, UK</p></div><div class="footer-links"><a href="about.html">About</a><a href="organization.html">Organization</a><a href="cfp.html">Call for Papers</a></div></div><div class="footnote">Building AI We Can Trust · Main conference: November 2 – 3, 2027</div></footer></body></html>'''
 (ROOT/f'{slug}.html').write_text(html)
print('Generated 9 static pages.')
